#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:22
msgid "Setting the region"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:24
msgid ""
"Many of the plotting methods take the ``region`` parameter, which sets the "
"area that will be shown in the figure. This tutorial covers the different "
"types of inputs that it can accept."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:30
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Axis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:39
msgid "Coordinates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:41
msgid ""
"A string of coordinates can be passed to ``region``, in the form of *xmin*/"
"*xmax*/*ymin*/*ymax*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:46
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Set the x-range from 10E to 20E and the y-range to 35N to 45N\n"
"    region=\"10/20/35/45\",\n"
"    # Set projection to Mercator, and the map width to 15 centimeters\n"
"    projection=\"M15c\",\n"
"    # Set the color of the land to light gray\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    # Set the color of the water to white\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    # Display the national borders and set the pen thickness to 0.5p\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    # Display the shorelines and set the pen thickness to 0.5p\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    # Set the frame to display annotations and gridlines\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:71
msgid ""
"The coordinates can be passed to ``region`` as a list, in the form of "
"[*xmin*, *xmax*, *ymin*, *ymax*]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:76
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Set the x-range from 10E to 20E and the y-range to 35N to 45N\n"
"    region=[10, 20, 35, 45],\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:95
msgid ""
"Instead of passing axes minima and maxima, the coordinates can be passed for "
"the bottom-left and top-right corners. The string format takes the "
"coordinates for the bottom-left and top-right coordinates. To specify corner "
"coordinates, append **+r** at the end of the ``region`` string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:102
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Set the bottom-left corner as 10E, 35N and the top-right corner as\n"
"    # 20E, 45N\n"
"    region=\"10/35/20/45+r\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:124
msgid "Global regions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:126
msgid ""
"In addition to passing coordinates, the argument **d** can be passed to set "
"the region to the entire globe. The range is 180W to 180E (-180, 180) and "
"90S to 90N (-90 to 90). With no parameters set for the projection, the map "
"defaults to be centered at the mid-point of both x- and y-axes. Using "
"**d**\\ , the map is centered at (0, 0), or the intersection of the equator "
"and prime meridian."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:135
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=\"d\",\n"
"    projection=\"Cyl_stere/12c\",\n"
"    land=\"darkgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:153
msgid ""
"The argument **g** can be passed, which encompasses the entire globe. The "
"range is 0E to 360E (0, 360) and 90S to 90N (-90 to 90). With no parameters "
"set for the projection, the map is centered at (180, 0), or the intersection "
"of the equator and International Date Line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:160
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=\"g\",\n"
"    projection=\"Cyl_stere/12c\",\n"
"    land=\"darkgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:180
msgid "ISO code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:182
msgid ""
"The ``region`` can be set to include a specific area specified by the two-"
"character ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 convention (for further information: https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_3166-1_alpha-2)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:188
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Set the map region to encompass Japan with the ISO code \"JP\"\n"
"    region=\"JP\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:207
msgid ""
"The area encompassed by the ISO code can be expanded by appending **+r**\\ "
"*increment* to the ISO code. The *increment* unit is in degrees, and if only "
"one value is added it expands the range of the region in all directions. "
"Using **+r** expands the final region boundaries to be multiples of "
"*increment* ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:215
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Expand the region boundaries to be multiples of 3 degrees in all\n"
"    # directions\n"
"    region=\"JP+r3\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:235
msgid ""
"Instead of expanding the range of the plot uniformly in all directions, two "
"values can be passed to expand differently on each axis. The format is "
"*xinc*/*yinc*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:241
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Expand the region boundaries to be multiples of 3 degrees on the x-"
"axis\n"
"    # and 5 degrees on the y-axis.\n"
"    region=\"JP+r3/5\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:261
msgid ""
"Instead of expanding the range of the plot uniformly in all directions, four "
"values can be passed to expand differently in each direction. The format is "
"*winc*/*einc*/*sinc*/*ninc*, which expands on the west, east, south, and "
"north axes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:268
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Expand the region boundaries to be multiples of 3 degrees to the west, "
"5\n"
"    # degrees to the east, 7 degrees to the south, and 9 degrees to the "
"north.\n"
"    region=\"JP+r3/5/7/9\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:288
msgid ""
"The ``region`` increment can be appended with **+R**, which adds the "
"increment without rounding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:293
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Expand the region setting outside the range of Japan by 3 degrees in "
"all\n"
"    # directions, without rounding to the nearest increment.\n"
"    region=\"JP+R3\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:313
msgid ""
"The ``region`` increment can be appended with **+e**, which is like **+r** "
"and expands the final region boundaries to be multiples of *increment*. "
"However, it ensures that the bounding box extends by at least 0.25 times the "
"increment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/regions.rst:320
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Expand the region boundaries to be multiples of 3 degrees in all\n"
"    # directions\n"
"    region=\"JP+e3\",\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",\n"
"    land=\"lightgray\",\n"
"    water=\"white\",\n"
"    borders=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.5p\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, grid=True),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

